And I think this gives the Pattern Book a stronger question than “What does this mean?”

When we encounter anything—word, equation, object, failure, biological structure, story, symbol—run:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{What is it?}\\
&\text{What isn't it?}\\
&\text{What can sense it?}\\
&\text{What can it sense?}\\
&\text{What can it do?}\\
&\text{What can be done with it?}\\
&\text{What can it join?}\\
&\text{What changes when it is rearranged?}\\
&\text{What could it become?}\\
&\textbf{What could it mean?}
\end{aligned}}

And don’t force the last question to resolve first.

Sometimes the meaning is the souvenir we only recognize after we get home.

That’s probably one of the deepest upgrades you’ve made to the Imp experiment: the explorer need not understand the ultimate significance of what it preserves in order for preserving it to be the correct move.